- request additional information from one or more of the merging parties;
- obtain an order from the Competition Tribunal to compel parties to provide information;
- determine that the merger is not likely to substantially prevent or lessen competition and end the review; or
- find that the merger is likely to substantially prevent or lessen competition and refer the merger to the Tribunal. The Bureau will not normally disclose information regarding the parties and the merger during the 45-day waiting period. What types of information are requested in a notification? The parties must provide the Bureau with certain information regarding the transaction and their operations. The Bureau will not normally request information regarding the purchaser where the vendor is disposing of its entire business in Canada or a substantial part of its business in Canada. The Bureau may request other information that is relevant to the review of the merger. The Bureau has issued Merger Notification Guidelines that set out the information that is normally requested by the Bureau. The Guidelines are available from the Competition Bureau or from the Bureau's website. Who pays for the cost of supplying information requested by the Bureau? Each party supplies the information that it has in its possession. Each party is responsible for its own out-of-pocket costs in preparing the information. The Bureau may, in appropriate circumstances, pay a party's costs of obtaining information, including the cost of retaining outside experts. What are the time limits in the merger notification process? There are no legislated time limits for the Bureau's review of a merger. In practice, the Bureau attempts to conduct its review within 120 days of receiving complete information. The Bureau will not normally extend the statutory waiting period of 45 days. If a merger is completed before the Bureau completes its review, the Bureau can obtain a court order to unscramble the merged firms. I have received a request for additional information from the Bureau. Is there any way that I can challenge the Bureau's request? The Bureau will normally not issue a request for additional information unless it is necessary to determine whether a merger is likely to substantially prevent or